He eats regularly at Wong's. Takes about twenty minutes for lunch, forty-five for dinner.

00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:07.130
He's due to good lunch any minute now.

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:09.690
I'd like to have a look at that new picture he's painting.

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:10.690
Mm-hmm.

00:23:10.690 --> 00:23:12.810
I've been playing around with the same idea.

00:23:12.810 --> 00:23:15.340
Twenty minutes doesn't give us much time.

00:23:15.340 --> 00:23:19.540
Do you think we could do it without giving ourselves away?

00:23:19.540 --> 00:23:20.780
Do we have a choice?

00:23:20.780 --> 00:23:21.700
Oh, Dan.

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:23.140
Yeah?

00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:24.240
He's leaving right now.

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:26.000
Okay, Potter.

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:30.000
Let's go.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:33.170
- Let us know when he gets to the restaurant. - Right.

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:34.290
He started his lunch. You better hurry.

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:36.690
Fine. Here we go.

00:25:05.180 --> 00:25:09.310
Quite good. You know, this brawn could be a pretty fair painter.

00:25:09.310 --> 00:25:11.310
Yes, if there wasn't so much red in these works.

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:14.550
That's right. It's a nice sense of perspective.

00:25:14.550 --> 00:25:18.350
I'll let you give me a lecture on art appreciation sometime...

00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:21.330
when we've got more than 20 minutes to do it in.

00:25:21.330 --> 00:25:22.810
I think we ought to get busy.

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:24.290
Merely a thought in passing.

00:25:41.960 --> 00:25:44.240
- There it is. - Yes, I see it.

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:47.640
Hold it steady. We're gonna want to check.

00:25:55.690 --> 00:25:57.370
The formula on the painting's new, all right.

00:25:57.530 --> 00:25:58.930
- You notice the difference? - Yes.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:02.110
I brought Lakeview people in for another nasty shock.

00:26:02.110 --> 00:26:03.110
Radshake came from Lakeview.

00:26:03.110 --> 00:26:03.960
Radshake saw Brawn.

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:04.960
Exit Radshake.

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:07.260
Brawn paints a new formula.

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:09.150
Hangs together very well, doesn't it?

00:26:09.150 --> 00:26:10.460
Too well.

00:26:10.460 --> 00:26:11.600
Senator, will you please?

00:26:11.600 --> 00:26:18.980
Yes, you're stretching 20 minutes awfully thin.

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:24.980
Yeah.

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:27.810
Having Mr. Brawn's fingerprints on file might prove useful someday.

00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:30.000
He's not attending a banquet, you know, is he?

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:33.840
- He's just grabbing a bite of lunch. - Never have a better chance than this.

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:45.420
- No good, huh? - No.

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:47.910
- Allison. - Tom's on his way.

00:26:47.910 --> 00:26:57.840
- Check. Here are some beauties.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:26:59.890
- All four things are the right hand. - Good.

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:14.690
That's it.

00:27:52.750 --> 00:27:59.660
On the following day Grayson and O'Hara watch Braun complete the painting which he created with great care.

00:28:02.810 --> 00:28:08.800
An express company truck called at the studio and Braun took a receipt for the painting from its driver.

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:26.340
Herra and Grayson followed the truck through the streets of San Francisco to its destination.

00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:50.300
they established the fact

00:28:50.300 --> 00:28:50.920
that the painting

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:51.740
was consigned

00:28:51.740 --> 00:28:52.900
to London, England.

00:28:53.820 --> 00:28:55.020
They decided not

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:56.120
to intercept this painting.

00:28:56.540 --> 00:28:57.240
They reasoned that

00:28:57.240 --> 00:28:57.780
such an act

00:28:57.780 --> 00:28:58.330
would be certain

00:28:58.330 --> 00:28:59.130
to alarm Braun

00:28:59.130 --> 00:29:00.000
before his contact

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:01.360
at Lakeview could be uncovered.

00:29:05.690 --> 00:29:09.120
That same afternoon, Igor Braun packed his bags

00:29:09.120 --> 00:29:10.840
for what seemed an extended journey.

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:13.200
He went to the San Francisco airport

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:15.740
and boarded a plane for Los Angeles.

00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:21.600
Flight 136, main line of Los Angeles loading at gate 3.

00:29:22.520 --> 00:29:27.490
Flight 136, main line of Los Angeles loading at gate 3.

00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:41.630
Without giving an indication of knowledge that he was being shadowed,

00:29:41.630 --> 00:29:46.960
Ron was met at the Los Angeles airport by a man driving an inconspicuous black sedan,

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:54.300
which led O'Hara and Grayson straight to Lakeview, California.

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:59.590
Johnson, come in, please.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:01.920
- Johnson to O'Hara, over.

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:04.320
- Cover the Lakeview Art Shop as quickly as possible.

00:30:04.320 --> 00:30:05.870
Airtight, understand?

00:30:05.870 --> 00:30:11.010
- Right.

00:30:11.010 --> 00:30:13.270
- Braun was received with respectful cordiality

00:30:13.270 --> 00:30:15.660
by the proprietor of the Lakeview Art Shop,

00:30:15.660 --> 00:30:18.260
1 Adolf Meisner.

00:30:18.260 --> 00:30:20.160
- I trust you had a pleasant trip, comrade?

00:30:20.160 --> 00:30:21.300
- Yes, thank you, Werner.

00:30:21.300 --> 00:30:27.530
- Fine, fine.

00:30:27.530 --> 00:30:28.350
- Good evening, comrade.

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:30.000
- Good evening, comrade Braun.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:33.180
- It's an honor to have you visit us, Comrade Bronn. - Thank you.

00:30:33.260 --> 00:30:35.540
We've been gathered to receive you since your phone call.

00:30:35.620 --> 00:30:39.520
It's good to see my friends again. Please.

00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:43.160
There's been a slight dislocation in our method of operation,

00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:45.850
which is responsible for my being here.

00:30:45.930 --> 00:30:49.610
- You refer to Comrade Radchick? - Yes.

00:30:49.690 --> 00:30:53.200
Radchick will not be with us again.

00:30:53.280 --> 00:30:56.400
Pity. He was guilty of stupidity and clumsiness

00:30:56.480 --> 00:30:59.480
in carrying out his last assignment.

00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:03.620
who are dedicated to the ideal of world revolution understand

00:31:03.680 --> 00:31:05.640
that the individual counts for nothing.

00:31:06.270 --> 00:31:09.720
We have the complete achievement of our work at Lakeview within our grasp.

00:31:10.170 --> 00:31:13.930
We cannot permit the slightest risk of jeopardizing our success.

00:31:15.170 --> 00:31:20.040
For that reason, I have decided to take personal command of the operation.

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:24.250
I promise you, the reward will be great.

00:31:24.350 --> 00:31:27.060
Well, that's wonderful. Your presence will be an inspiration.

00:31:27.300 --> 00:31:30.000
- Comrade Braun. - Yes, Comrade Christopher.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:32.040
About Radchek's misfortune.

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:37.050
I took this picture of the FBI man at Colton's liquidation...

00:31:37.050 --> 00:31:40.740
...a moment after the police discovered his body.

00:31:40.740 --> 00:31:41.600
Really?

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:43.600
The two gentlemen in civilian clothes.

00:31:43.600 --> 00:31:47.030
The one on the right is taking charge of the investigation.

00:31:47.030 --> 00:31:51.920
Interesting. Most interesting.

00:31:51.920 --> 00:31:54.380
Splendid work on your part, comrade.

00:31:54.380 --> 00:31:58.120
You must always remember, comrades...

00:31:58.120 --> 00:32:00.000
...it's advice.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:03.470
It's of vital importance to know your enemy.

00:32:03.570 --> 00:32:07.780
But it's of even greater importance that the enemy does not know you.

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:11.150
Radchick made that mistake.

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:13.830
I trust there will be no more.

00:32:13.930 --> 00:32:16.910
We will always cherish Radchick's bemini.

00:32:17.890 --> 00:32:22.570
And we will not forget this gentleman.

00:32:23.270 --> 00:32:26.080
With Braun and the art shop under complete surveillance.

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:29.590
O'Hara and Grayson next conferred with Dr. Frederick Townsend.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:32.500
of the Lakeview Nuclear Research Laboratory.

00:32:32.500 --> 00:32:34.610
The only person connected with the project

00:32:34.610 --> 00:32:37.610
who knew O'Hara to be an agent of the F.B.I.

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:43.800
- Happy to see you again, Mr. O'Hara. - Thank you, Dr. Townsend.

00:32:43.800 --> 00:32:46.900
Doctor, this is Mr. Grayson, Scott and Yacht.

00:32:46.900 --> 00:32:47.820
Mr. Grayson, Doctor.

00:32:47.820 --> 00:32:50.320
Because of the international ramifications of this matter,

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:52.820
Mr. Grayson has been put on special assignment

00:32:52.820 --> 00:32:54.130
to work with me.

00:32:54.130 --> 00:32:56.630
Something on the order of reverse lend-lease, Doctor.

00:32:56.630 --> 00:33:00.000
As a matter of fact, Doctor, it was Mr. Grayson here who uncovered it.

00:33:00.190 --> 00:33:04.450
Well, naturally, what you've discovered comes as a tremendous shock to me.

00:33:04.550 --> 00:33:07.350
Even in the face of such incontrovertible proof,

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:09.850
I can hardly bring myself to believe it.

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:13.570
Just how serious is it, Doctor, from the standpoint of your work?

00:33:13.670 --> 00:33:18.370
It's more than serious, Mr. Grayson. It's almost fatal.

00:33:18.470 --> 00:33:21.530
We are coming very near to the completion of our project.

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:25.030
And this formula, which found its way outside this plant,

00:33:25.130 --> 00:33:29.130
represents a vital stage in the structure of our research.

00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:39.180
I hate to add to your distress, doctor, but since that time, another formula has found its way in the wrong hands.

00:33:41.480 --> 00:33:45.530
Until a week ago, this formula had not been conceived.

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:49.080
Whoever is guilty has a rapid and direct line of communication.

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:57.860
Well, it establishes, gentlemen, that up to this minute, the ultimate recipient of this stolen information knows as much about our work as I do.

00:33:58.430 --> 00:34:00.000
I'm afraid that's the only conclusion, doctor.

00:34:00.540 --> 00:34:02.210
That's a monstrous thought.

00:34:02.290 --> 00:34:06.660
One of the few of us in this laboratory who share such knowledge is a traitor.

00:34:07.430 --> 00:34:10.050
I understand the urgency of your task, gentlemen.

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:11.450
Where would you like to begin?

00:34:11.530 --> 00:34:15.740
Well, for my own satisfaction and because Mr. Grayson is unfamiliar with it...

00:34:15.850 --> 00:34:18.580
I'd like to recheck the physical security procedure.

00:34:18.780 --> 00:34:22.280
Very well. This way, please.

00:34:27.320 --> 00:34:30.000
O'Hara and Grayson search for a flaw in the security...

00:34:30.060 --> 00:34:39.490
regulations as they toured the plant with Dr. Townsend. The guards were

00:34:39.490 --> 00:34:44.250
specially chosen men, each of them an ex-soldier, sailor or marine, who had

00:34:44.250 --> 00:34:52.800
proven his loyalty and love of country and battle. The workers disrobed in a

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:56.810
special guarded locker room and then stripped of the hide passed into a

00:34:56.810 --> 00:35:00.000
second locker room in which they donned standardized working

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:05.160
clothes before being permitted to proceed to their posts. This routine was mandatory

00:35:05.160 --> 00:35:11.510
for every worker entering or leaving the plant. As an additional safeguard, it was necessary

00:35:11.510 --> 00:35:16.010
to submit to the search of this electronic eye, which could detect the presence of the

00:35:16.010 --> 00:35:20.090
most minute particle of metal upon the person of anyone passing before it.

00:35:20.090 --> 00:35:26.170
Well, there you are. You've seen it all. What do you think? Armed guards, barbed wire,

00:35:26.170 --> 00:35:30.000
electronics? Seems pretty tight. Evidently not tight.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:41.180
As I understand it, the creative brains here are your colleagues Alan, Von Stob, Forrest and, of course, yourself.

00:35:41.180 --> 00:35:48.570
With Dr. Neva acting in the capacity of a confidential assistant and sort of a recording secretary.

00:35:48.570 --> 00:35:49.570
That's correct.

00:35:49.570 --> 00:35:53.490
Do all of you know about everything? I mean, do you do your thinking together or is it every man for himself?

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:58.410
Well, each of us works on a different phase of the problem, independent of the others.

00:35:58.410 --> 00:36:00.000
Then, once a week, we'll do it.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:05.300
usually on Friday we hold a conference at which we pool all the developments of

00:36:05.300 --> 00:36:11.060
the week our progress is evaluated and everyone's brought up to date I see then

00:36:11.060 --> 00:36:14.650
every Friday for the moment anyway everybody does know everything exactly

00:36:14.650 --> 00:36:22.730
where do you hold your conferences doctor right here in this room fun as you can

00:36:22.730 --> 00:36:30.000
see this also contains the special vault where all our data is kept mm-hmm does dr.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:33.910
- Does Neva take part in these conferences? - Certainly, the most important part.

00:36:33.910 --> 00:36:35.910
She takes notes of everything.

00:36:35.910 --> 00:36:39.790
Dr. Neva is practically the hub around which our wheel revolves.

00:36:39.790 --> 00:36:42.220
She's a most remarkable girl.

00:36:42.220 --> 00:36:44.720
She must be to hold such an important position.

00:36:44.720 --> 00:36:48.920
- Is this the door that leads to your private study, Doctor? - Yes.

00:36:48.920 --> 00:36:52.920
- Is it ever used during the conferences? - No, that's always kept locked.

00:36:52.920 --> 00:36:54.920
- I have the only key to that. - I see.

00:36:54.920 --> 00:36:56.310
One more question, sir.

00:36:56.310 --> 00:36:59.990
Are you and your associate scientists permitted to leave the premises?

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.000
It's not a penitentiary, Mr. Griesens.

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:03.910
We're perfectly free to come and go as we wish,

00:37:03.910 --> 00:37:05.890
governed by the same security regulations

00:37:05.890 --> 00:37:07.890
that apply to everyone who works on the project.

00:37:07.890 --> 00:37:09.980
Well, thank you, sir.

00:37:09.980 --> 00:37:11.870
Yes, thanks very much, Doctor.

00:37:11.870 --> 00:37:13.760
We won't take any more of your time now.

00:37:13.760 --> 00:37:15.680
You've been most cooperative. It's been a pleasure.

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:20.000
With the cooperation of Dr. Townsend,

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:22.080
the mirror in his study door

00:37:22.080 --> 00:37:24.080
was secretly replaced

00:37:24.080 --> 00:37:26.090
with a special type of glass

00:37:26.090 --> 00:37:28.090
through which they could observe and photograph

00:37:28.090 --> 00:37:30.000
every move made at the Friday conference.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:35.580
conference, but which could not be seen through from the conference room side. For the first

00:37:35.580 --> 00:37:41.750
time, Grayson and O'Hara could observe the key figures of the Lakeview project in action.

00:37:41.750 --> 00:37:47.830
First there was Dr. Ritter von Stolb of Viennese, formerly professor of nuclear physics at Heidelberg,

00:37:47.830 --> 00:37:53.180
and an internationally famous mathematician. After the Allied victory in Europe, Dr. von

00:37:53.180 --> 00:38:00.000
Stolb was persuaded to come to the United States to give our government the benefit of his knowledge.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.580
There was Dr. Homer Allen, physicist and expert on rocket propulsion,

00:38:04.580 --> 00:38:10.440
an American whose lineage could be traced back to the original settlers of the Massachusetts Bay Colony.

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:15.270
Then there was Dr. William Forrest, English scientist,

00:38:15.270 --> 00:38:20.420
whose experiments with heavy water contributed greatly to the final achievement of atomic fission.

00:38:20.420 --> 00:38:25.200
He was on leave from the British Atomic Commission to the Lakeview Project.

00:38:25.200 --> 00:38:29.830
Last, but far from least, there was Dr. Anastasia Neva,

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:34.210
Tony, for short, Doctor of Philosophy at the University of Michigan,

00:38:34.310 --> 00:38:37.100
mistress of five languages, including Russian,

00:38:37.200 --> 00:38:40.390
and Townsend's secretary and confidential assistant.

00:38:40.470 --> 00:38:44.710
In addition to being photographed,

00:38:44.790 --> 00:38:48.820
a wire recorder preserved every word uttered within the room.

00:38:48.880 --> 00:38:51.350
Now, Doctor, the point that still baffles us

00:38:51.450 --> 00:38:53.650
is the ratio of energy to be released

00:38:53.750 --> 00:38:56.650
in offsetting the dynamic deceleration of the carrier vehicle.

00:38:56.750 --> 00:38:58.950
Yes, I know. Von Stowe have been working on that.

00:38:59.070 --> 00:39:00.000
If there is a solution,

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:02.000
it's purely mathematical.

00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:04.930
Theoretical, rather. Almost fantasy.

00:39:04.930 --> 00:39:09.590
My dear Alan, mathematics is a pure science

00:39:09.590 --> 00:39:11.580
which doesn't permit fantasy.

00:39:11.580 --> 00:39:13.480
Only fact.

00:39:13.480 --> 00:39:15.480
Eventually, all theory must be submitted

00:39:15.480 --> 00:39:17.480
to the proof of the law of numbers,

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:20.670
which, I'm happy to say,

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:23.670
has established our theory to be factual.

00:39:23.670 --> 00:39:29.380
- Here. - This is amazing.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:32.580
This is certainly the answer to our problem.

00:39:33.080 --> 00:39:35.300
Mathematics may be completely factual, as you say,

00:39:35.380 --> 00:39:38.420
but in your case, it's certainly blessed with a tremendous imagination.

00:39:38.500 --> 00:39:41.760
In the beginning, Einstein's entire thesis was imagination.

00:39:41.840 --> 00:39:44.540
- I'm so proud of you, Ritter. - Thank you, darling.

00:39:45.330 --> 00:39:48.590
Well, isn't our doubting Thomas convinced?

00:39:49.090 --> 00:39:53.240
Stove, I don't quite understand the application of your second equation.

00:39:53.250 --> 00:39:55.170
Oh, I do. It's beautifully established.

00:39:55.250 --> 00:39:58.560
I may be mistaken, but it strikes me as being a minus factor.

00:39:58.930 --> 00:40:00.000
You have missed the inclusion

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:04.940
of the element N3 in the reconstruction of the nuclear pattern.

00:40:05.030 --> 00:40:09.960
Oh, yeah. I should have known you couldn't be guilty of error.

00:40:10.060 --> 00:40:11.460
Oh, don't say that.

00:40:11.560 --> 00:40:15.760
Again, our mathematical Babe Ruth hits a home run with the bases full.

00:40:15.860 --> 00:40:19.360
Now, that's what I would like to do. To play baseball.

00:40:19.460 --> 00:40:21.360
Well, our game is nearly over.

00:40:21.410 --> 00:40:24.280
One more inning like this, and I think we shall have won it.

00:40:24.360 --> 00:40:27.560
Care to have dinner with me tonight and a game of chess, really?

00:40:27.560 --> 00:40:30.000
I think I have an engagement for David.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:34.800
- No, thanks very much. - He has an engagement with Kirsten.

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:38.320
- You'll take care of the notes, don't you? - Oh, yes, of course, Doctor.

00:41:09.280 --> 00:41:10.850
All right, that's enough, Dawson.

00:41:11.560 --> 00:41:14.310
Well, there we saw it. Genius at work.

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:17.620
Notice anything out of the ordinary?

00:41:17.740 --> 00:41:20.710
No, except the little scene between Von Stolbe and Dr. Neva...

00:41:21.070 --> 00:41:23.270
and the fact that she was the last person to handle the paper...

00:41:23.390 --> 00:41:26.440
on which he wrote his equation, if that's out of the ordinary.

00:41:26.960 --> 00:41:30.000
Yet it's right after these conferences that information is slated...

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:38.400
the brawn. Close examination of the film failed to provide a lead to the manner in which information

00:41:38.400 --> 00:41:43.280
was being spirited out of Lakeview, but deduction convinced both O'Hara and Grayson that the time

00:41:43.280 --> 00:41:49.550
immediately following the Friday conferences was the hotspot. Acting on this reasoning, O'Hara took

00:41:49.550 --> 00:41:55.750
immediate steps to have all of the key figures of the Lakeview project shadowed. All right, Malone.

00:41:55.750 --> 00:41:57.680
Okay, Dan, I've got him.

00:42:23.870 --> 00:42:24.750
Who's driving them?

00:42:24.750 --> 00:42:27.310
Thompson. He's a pretty good cab driver, isn't he?

00:42:27.310 --> 00:42:28.480
Excellent.

00:43:54.120 --> 00:43:55.250
Myra speaking.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.910
- Take him alone, go to bed. - No luck, huh?

00:44:02.990 --> 00:44:07.140
No. Townsend made a speech at the professional club in Glendale.

00:44:07.620 --> 00:44:12.540
Von Stobbe. The place to take your girl. Hollywood Bowl.

00:44:12.680 --> 00:44:15.310
Wagner. Tristan and the cylinder.

00:44:15.390 --> 00:44:17.090
You couldn't say it wasn't romantic.

00:44:17.190 --> 00:44:21.020
You couldn't say Von Stobbe and Dr. Neva went in the mood.

00:44:24.170 --> 00:44:29.510
- Hello? - Yes, this is Dan.

00:44:31.500 --> 00:44:33.140
All right, Thompson, call it a day.

00:44:34.630 --> 00:44:37.950
Forrest and Allen had dinner alone at the prime rib.

00:44:38.760 --> 00:44:40.840
Talked science, then went back to the plant, period.

00:44:45.880 --> 00:44:58.050
Snore, Scotty?

00:44:58.950 --> 00:45:00.000
All Englishmen snore.

00:45:01.120 --> 00:45:04.880
If I should tap you lightly on the head during the night with an ashtray, you'll know what I mean.

00:45:06.180 --> 00:45:07.810
Oh, boy, am I tired.

00:45:10.920 --> 00:45:12.780
Nice people are scientific suspects.

00:45:13.660 --> 00:45:15.200
Very nice. Very congenial.

00:45:16.090 --> 00:45:19.830
Except one or more is a traitor and an accessory before the fact of two murders.

00:45:21.200 --> 00:45:22.860
Care to hazard a guess, Mr. Grayson?

00:45:23.370 --> 00:45:24.250
That's the rub, chum.

00:45:24.470 --> 00:45:25.930
You pay your money, it takes your choice.

00:45:26.570 --> 00:45:27.530
I'll take Tony.

00:45:28.510 --> 00:45:29.590
She is rather attractive.

00:45:30.890 --> 00:45:33.810
Von Staub does all right for a scientist.

00:45:33.840 --> 00:45:35.440
Romance among the research.

00:45:36.520 --> 00:45:40.310
Well, I suppose it can happen, even in such sterilized environment.

00:45:40.600 --> 00:45:42.230
Yes, but what does it prove?

00:45:42.520 --> 00:45:47.380
It seems to prove that Von Staub mixes a few red corpuscles with his protons and neutrons.

00:45:47.410 --> 00:45:51.380
So what? She had my red corpuscles playing leapfrog, too.

00:45:51.890 --> 00:45:55.440
Your red corpuscles are probably in a constant state of agitation.

00:45:56.040 --> 00:45:57.490
Is that bad?

00:46:01.980 --> 00:46:05.000
You know, if a man like Townsend can be a traitor to his country,

00:46:05.100 --> 00:46:07.300
I'm ready to resign from the human race.

00:46:08.060 --> 00:46:10.370
Yes, I feel the same about Forrest.

00:46:11.010 --> 00:46:14.080
How about Allen? Pioneer American stock?

00:46:15.040 --> 00:46:17.080
Union League club? Boston Park Bay?

00:46:17.540 --> 00:46:20.390
Oh, fine. We're practically proving what has happened

00:46:20.390 --> 00:46:21.630
couldn't possibly have happened.

00:46:22.100 --> 00:46:24.300
We've eliminated ourselves completely out of suspects.

00:46:26.810 --> 00:46:27.770
You know what I think?

00:46:28.190 --> 00:46:28.490
How?

00:46:29.060 --> 00:46:30.000
I think I need a good...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:33.300
Good night. Sleep. Tackle this thing with a fresh mind in the morning.

00:46:33.300 --> 00:46:37.890
- Good night. - Night.

00:46:37.890 --> 00:46:48.320
O'Hara, my all red corpuscles are bouncing in pursuit of the dream image of the fair Tony.

00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:51.630
It might be well to add these few random thoughts of her to your dreams.

00:46:51.630 --> 00:46:55.800
- Why don't you go to sleep? - Item.

00:46:55.800 --> 00:46:59.960
She is Townsend's confidential secretary and is completely informed on all phases of the Lakefield.

00:47:00.160 --> 00:47:04.930
Item. Only she, besides Townsend, knows the combination to the vault.

00:47:04.930 --> 00:47:08.720
Item. She speaks five languages, including Russian.

00:47:08.720 --> 00:47:13.090
Interesting, isn't it?

00:47:13.090 --> 00:47:17.010
Well, good night.

00:47:17.010 --> 00:47:25.990
Wait a minute. Miss, what did you mean about Tony?

00:47:25.990 --> 00:47:30.000
Huh? Well, that's what I keep asking.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:33.810
I'm asking myself, "Just what do I mean?"

00:47:33.810 --> 00:47:42.290
Oh, we'll approach it with a fresh mind in the morning.

00:47:42.290 --> 00:47:52.980
On the Monday following the Friday conference of Lakeview scientists...

00:47:52.980 --> 00:47:56.920
...Egor Braun expressed another great address to London, England...

00:47:56.920 --> 00:48:00.000
...acting under special authority.

00:48:00.120 --> 00:48:06.660
Grayson and O'Hara boarded the train, opened the crate, and established that it contained another painting by Braun.

00:48:10.970 --> 00:48:16.770
Later the same night, the painting was examined in the laboratory of the Los Angeles office of the FBI.

00:48:24.050 --> 00:48:30.000
I hope Dr. Townsend hasn't a weak heart. This is going to be an even greater show.

00:48:30.640 --> 00:48:32.310
And we'll have to let it go through.

00:48:32.390 --> 00:48:35.720
To hold it any length of time is almost certain to arouse brawn suspicions.

00:48:35.820 --> 00:48:36.560
That's right.

00:48:36.940 --> 00:48:40.120
We mustn't do that until we know how he gets the data out of the plant.

00:48:40.240 --> 00:48:41.780
And who's supplying him with it.

00:48:42.510 --> 00:48:46.990
I'd be willing to take the stand and swear that nothing can get out of that project but the laundry.

00:48:47.090 --> 00:48:54.450
- What was that again? - I said nothing can get out of there but the laundry.

00:48:54.540 --> 00:48:56.400
You're so right.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:01.840
Here it is.

00:49:01.840 --> 00:49:04.610
Report on local people who visited Braun.

00:49:04.610 --> 00:49:06.630
The first name on the list is Carl Benesch,

00:49:06.630 --> 00:49:09.420
night manager of the elite laundry.

00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:10.820
You see that?

00:49:10.820 --> 00:49:15.830
Sure.

00:49:15.830 --> 00:49:17.880
The elite laundry.

00:49:17.880 --> 00:49:20.680
Friday night after the conference, Tony and Von Staub.

00:49:20.680 --> 00:49:22.070
Quiet.

00:49:22.070 --> 00:49:24.740
Looks like I'm about to get a new job in the laundry.

00:49:24.740 --> 00:49:27.480
I think it's a job for Grayson, if he'll take it on him.

00:49:27.480 --> 00:49:30.000
It will do away with even the slightest risk of having you or

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:33.270
One of our other agents recognized by anybody in the laundry.

00:49:33.350 --> 00:49:35.010
- How about it? - Of course I'll take it out.

00:49:35.090 --> 00:49:39.830
Good.

00:49:39.910 --> 00:49:44.650
The Federal Bureau of Investigation arranged to have Grayson planted in the elite laundry.

00:49:44.750 --> 00:49:47.320
Where, on the following Friday night at 8 o'clock...

00:51:23.230 --> 00:51:25.330
because of the fact that the Lakeview project

00:51:25.330 --> 00:51:27.720
was within one step of completion.

00:51:27.720 --> 00:51:30.000
It had been decided that no more of the secret data

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:32.800
could be permitted to fall into the hands of Egor Braun.

00:51:33.080 --> 00:51:35.760
Hance O'Hara was forced to take direct action.

00:52:39.760 --> 00:52:52.090
- Check that for Leighton Prince, will ya? - Right.

00:52:52.190 --> 00:52:55.670
Strange, he laid his handkerchief and a bundle of men's clothes.

00:52:55.770 --> 00:53:00.000
You don't know just how strange it is.

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:06.100
stitched hand embroidered Irish linen never been laundered Fred take a look at

00:53:06.100 --> 00:53:09.860
this for writing will you okay let's see what it shows under heat

00:53:33.130 --> 00:53:35.480
- Let's try ammonia fumes. - Go ahead.

00:54:07.000 --> 00:54:08.130
- What do you think?

00:54:08.130 --> 00:54:10.500
- Doesn't look like it's gonna work.

00:54:10.500 --> 00:54:11.720
- Boys better come up with something

00:54:11.720 --> 00:54:13.690
or I'm a dead duck.

00:54:13.690 --> 00:54:15.250
- We'll go to chemicals.

00:54:15.250 --> 00:54:16.090
- It may take some time.

00:54:16.090 --> 00:54:20.800
- I'll wait.

00:54:20.800 --> 00:54:25.960
- Come right by there.

00:54:25.960 --> 00:54:28.600
- Yes, come right, bro.

00:54:28.600 --> 00:54:29.570
- Where is Krebs?

00:54:30.600 --> 00:54:32.850
- He's never been this late. - Find out if he's left the laundry.

00:54:32.850 --> 00:54:47.140
- This immediately. - Maybe it's a silver salt solution.

00:54:47.220 --> 00:54:48.310
- Well, let's try for it.

00:55:18.200 --> 00:55:23.140
- Yeah, she blows. - That's it. That's what I was looking for.

00:55:24.440 --> 00:55:26.020
Now that we've got it, how was it done?

00:55:26.740 --> 00:55:30.000
With the draftsman's round-pointed pen, using a solution containing silver salt...

00:55:30.920 --> 00:55:33.440
It's the same stuff that's used in the mulch in a photographic film.

00:55:33.600 --> 00:55:35.780
Then, to bring it out, you treat it with a developer.

00:55:36.060 --> 00:55:37.420
That turns a silversault's plague.

00:55:39.660 --> 00:55:40.820
Yes, it all checks.

00:55:41.870 --> 00:55:43.150
Oh, thanks, fellas. Thanks a lot.

00:55:43.850 --> 00:55:46.980
Oh, Fred, will you bake that out on your cook stove and shoot it down in my office?

00:55:47.220 --> 00:55:47.820
Okay, Dan.

00:55:48.270 --> 00:55:48.740
See you later.

00:55:51.200 --> 00:55:57.490
So, someone took the package of laundry away from you, comrade Krebs.

00:55:58.380 --> 00:55:59.240
Yes.

00:55:59.240 --> 00:55:59.480
Yes.

00:56:00.350 --> 00:56:03.500
In the alley, in the dark. I had no chance.

00:56:03.580 --> 00:56:05.610
Do you realize what you're saying?

00:56:05.690 --> 00:56:09.540
I... I'm trying to explain how it was.

00:56:10.120 --> 00:56:14.600
Will you also explain how, at the very moment of our success,

00:56:14.680 --> 00:56:17.090
you've brought us to the brink of disaster?

00:56:17.290 --> 00:56:20.210
You are guilty of worse stupidity and greater negligence

00:56:20.290 --> 00:56:23.490
- than the late comrade Ratchett. - Oh, but comrade, I...

00:56:25.330 --> 00:56:28.280
The man who took the bundle knew its contents.

00:56:28.360 --> 00:56:30.000
He must have followed you from the laundry.

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:34.110
- Who was that man? - I don't know, Comrade.

00:56:34.210 --> 00:56:36.590
I don't know. I didn't have a chance to see.

00:56:36.690 --> 00:56:39.650
- Comrade Braun. - Yeah?

00:56:39.750 --> 00:56:42.530
- There's a man at the laundry, a new man. - What new man?

00:56:42.630 --> 00:56:44.530
He came to work Tuesday on the night shift.

00:56:44.630 --> 00:56:47.440
- What about him? - Well, it's nothing you can pick out,

00:56:47.540 --> 00:56:50.940
but he doesn't seem like the other laundry floaters that take jobs like this.

00:56:51.040 --> 00:56:54.990
I see. And you waited until now to tell me about this new man?

00:56:55.090 --> 00:56:58.200
- Well, I wasn't sure. - No, you weren't.

00:57:00.410 --> 00:57:01.450
We'll make sure.

00:57:08.230 --> 00:57:12.790
Grayson had taken a cheap room in the poor and almost deserted section of town...

00:57:13.240 --> 00:57:16.200
...as being more in keeping with his role of laundry worker.

00:57:36.490 --> 00:57:38.970
Mutual, 72419, Los Angeles, please.

00:57:39.060 --> 00:57:50.200
Sir Dan O'Hara, please.

00:58:00.570 --> 00:58:05.080
- I'm glad I caught you. I have a call for you from Lakeview. - Thank you.

00:58:05.160 --> 00:58:10.800
Pardon my curiosity, Mr. O'Hara, but I'd like to keep myself posted on things.

00:58:10.880 --> 00:58:15.630
- How about the laundry? - Scotty, I think we've hit the jackpot.

00:58:15.680 --> 00:58:17.670
The answer to the winner-take-all question.

00:58:17.750 --> 00:58:21.210
I'm on my way out to Lakeview now with the evidence to talk to Dr. Townsend.

00:58:21.250 --> 00:58:24.490
- Where are you? - Oh, I... I'm at home.

00:58:24.520 --> 00:58:28.440
I'll pick you up on my way out. Be then about to 25 minutes.

00:58:28.520 --> 00:58:30.000
Right. Uh, be careful of that.

00:58:30.090 --> 00:58:53.620
- They're very tired. - Snap it!

00:58:53.700 --> 00:59:00.000
- Right into it, eh? - Sure.

00:59:01.280 --> 00:59:03.940
- Ivan, you and Feodor stay outside. - Right.

00:59:04.530 --> 00:59:09.090
- Keep out of sight. - Look, my friend.

00:59:09.370 --> 00:59:12.320
Nobody in the house but you and the landlady here.

00:59:12.720 --> 00:59:15.980
So don't worry about being disturbed.

00:59:16.460 --> 00:59:19.650
Now, just who are you?

00:59:19.800 --> 00:59:22.130
Do you always introduce yourself to strangers like this?

00:59:22.210 --> 00:59:24.750
- Your name is Grayson, isn't it? - I'm sorry. You have the advantage of me.

00:59:24.830 --> 00:59:27.670
Look at his hands. It's like I said. He never did a day's work before in his life.

00:59:27.750 --> 00:59:30.000
I wouldn't say that.

00:59:30.760 --> 00:59:33.840
- Maybe you think we're playing games. - I wouldn't say that, either.

00:59:34.370 --> 00:59:36.210
See if he has anything on him.

00:59:44.450 --> 00:59:49.140
- Why'd you go to work for the laundry? - My appetite.

00:59:53.080 --> 00:59:57.200
You sound like you're British. What are you doing in Lakeview?

00:59:57.710 --> 01:00:00.000
I came here for my health.

01:00:28.540 --> 01:00:29.600
Hey, pay attention.

01:00:30.510 --> 01:00:31.750
Have you ever seen this man before?

01:00:33.180 --> 01:00:34.890
You're lying.

01:00:35.930 --> 01:00:37.530
Maybe you're an FBI man, too.

01:00:38.100 --> 01:00:38.930
I'm not.

01:00:39.640 --> 01:00:41.680
You were planted in that laundry for a reason.

01:00:42.660 --> 01:00:43.260
What was it?

01:00:43.720 --> 01:00:46.060
I don't know what you're talking about.

01:00:46.300 --> 01:00:48.100
You saw me pick up a bundle of laundry tonight.

01:00:48.710 --> 01:00:50.090
Tipped off your G-man partner.

01:00:51.050 --> 01:00:52.110
What did he want with that bundle?

01:00:52.620 --> 01:00:53.340
Where'd he take it?

01:00:53.640 --> 01:00:55.280
I still don't know what you're talking about.

01:00:55.380 --> 01:00:56.240
You can get rough, a fella.

01:00:56.500 --> 01:00:56.980
Wait a minute.

01:00:58.640 --> 01:01:00.000
Maybe we can find out another one.

01:01:05.210 --> 01:01:09.070
Look, I'm going to ask you a couple of questions.

01:01:09.150 --> 01:01:10.660
The answers better be right.

01:01:11.050 --> 01:01:12.700
How long have you known this man?

01:01:12.710 --> 01:01:14.690
Came to live here a week ago.

01:01:14.760 --> 01:01:17.060
Has anyone come to visit him since he's been here?

01:01:17.770 --> 01:01:19.400
Nobody.

01:01:20.500 --> 01:01:27.720
I'll keep on refreshing your memory like that.

01:01:27.780 --> 01:01:30.000
Now, who did he talk to in the television?

01:01:30.000 --> 01:01:33.530
- Who called him? - I don't know.

01:01:33.530 --> 01:01:42.100
- Have you ever seen this man around here? - No.

01:01:42.100 --> 01:01:45.240
You're lying. He did come here. He came to see him.

01:01:45.240 --> 01:01:49.060
- No. - Come on, you can do better than that.

01:01:49.060 --> 01:01:52.970
You're stalling. You're stalling!

01:01:56.790 --> 01:01:57.650
Relax!

01:01:57.650 --> 01:02:00.000
Don't worry about me.

01:02:01.310 --> 01:02:06.640
I see plenty fellows like these before I escape to this country.

01:02:06.640 --> 01:02:11.540
This is not the first time I've been asked questions in the same way

01:02:11.540 --> 01:02:14.270
by gentlemen like him.

01:02:14.270 --> 01:02:19.620
My whole family was questioned to death.

01:02:19.620 --> 01:02:22.760
I'm the only one left to answer more questions.

01:03:03.070 --> 01:03:04.680
- You don't have to knock. - Go right in.

01:03:11.000 --> 01:03:14.980
It isn't a surprise.

01:03:21.200 --> 01:03:26.620
It's just part of what I owe you for that business in the alley.

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:32.150
Come out, aren't you, sir? You found the answer.

01:03:33.150 --> 01:03:36.830
Thank you for delivering it to me in person.

01:03:38.150 --> 01:03:40.430
This is the man you said you didn't know.

01:03:41.270 --> 01:03:43.700
You didn't know him either. He never came here.

01:03:44.520 --> 01:03:47.730
All right, Curly. Give us 10 minutes.

01:03:47.760 --> 01:03:49.860
And you and Feodor remove these gentlemen.

01:03:49.960 --> 01:03:52.150
I see. When you can't convince, you kill, eh?

01:03:52.180 --> 01:03:55.150
We have a nicer word for it. Liquidation.

01:03:55.520 --> 01:03:56.820
- You know what to do. - Sure.

01:03:56.920 --> 01:03:58.880
Just make sure they have no identification on them.

01:03:59.260 --> 01:04:00.000
All right, Benish.

01:04:04.090 --> 01:04:07.970
- Well, goodbye, gentlemen.

01:04:07.970 --> 01:04:10.250
- All right, on your feet.

01:04:10.250 --> 01:04:11.620
You too.

01:04:11.620 --> 01:04:13.410
Come on, get going.

01:04:13.410 --> 01:04:16.710
Stand over against the wall.

01:04:16.710 --> 01:04:20.500
Put your hands over your heads.

01:04:20.500 --> 01:04:23.860
Big boy, turn around.

01:04:23.860 --> 01:04:26.260
Slip off your coat.

01:04:26.260 --> 01:04:28.940
I'll get those cleaners' tags.

01:04:28.940 --> 01:04:30.000
- Haven't had it that long.

01:04:30.760 --> 01:04:33.260
Too bad you won't be able to wear it longer.

01:04:33.260 --> 01:04:35.260
And no funny business when you throw it.

01:04:35.260 --> 01:04:42.180
Watch. Yours too.

01:04:42.180 --> 01:04:49.040
I'll take that pen and pencil set too.

01:05:25.600 --> 01:05:30.000
This is your good man, and I know from what those others

01:05:30.060 --> 01:05:36.690
said what they are fighting for I'll call an ambulance no because I will not be here when it

01:05:36.690 --> 01:05:47.000
comes I am happy I can do such a small thing for my country country that has done so much for me

01:06:00.120 --> 01:06:02.880
Immediately after seeing their two prisoners jailed,

01:06:02.880 --> 01:06:05.570
O'Hara and Grayson raided the Lakeview art shop.

01:06:05.570 --> 01:06:07.660
Boys have got the back door covered.

01:06:07.660 --> 01:06:10.060
Good. You fellows cover the alley. We'll take the front.

01:06:10.060 --> 01:06:12.160
The capture of Drawn and Krebs,

01:06:12.160 --> 01:06:16.160
and the recovery of the handkerchief containing a copy of the secret formula,

01:06:16.160 --> 01:06:18.470
was now of paramount importance.

01:07:19.360 --> 01:07:24.500
clean getaway to show himself to the men we had watching i didn't think he'd be foolish enough

01:07:24.500 --> 01:07:29.550
to stay here and greet us wonder where he's gone to wait for the final formula that i don't know

01:07:30.190 --> 01:07:31.640
I do know he's not gonna get it.

01:07:36.500 --> 01:07:39.640
There's Von Stob and Tony Guilty.

01:07:39.680 --> 01:07:42.070
That's incredible. It's fantastic.

01:07:42.170 --> 01:07:44.150
How far has your work progressed at this moment?

01:07:44.650 --> 01:07:47.640
Dr. Von Stob provided us with the final formula last night.

01:07:48.060 --> 01:07:51.590
Has either Tony or Von Stob left the project since that time?

01:07:51.690 --> 01:07:53.170
No, none of us.

01:07:54.620 --> 01:07:56.500
- Good morning. - Good morning.

01:07:56.760 --> 01:07:58.740
- You send for me, Dr. Townsend? - Yes.

01:07:59.040 --> 01:08:00.000
This is Mr. O'Hara.

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:04.000
- Federal Bureau of Investigation. This is Mr. Grayson, Scotland Yard. - How do you do?

01:08:04.070 --> 01:08:07.500
- How do you do? - They'd like to talk to you privately.

01:08:07.600 --> 01:08:11.840
- Privately? Is anything wrong? - Yes, I'm afraid there is.

01:08:11.890 --> 01:08:16.570
- Would you step into the study, please? - Yes. Yes, certainly.

01:08:16.610 --> 01:08:21.020
Doctor, I'd like to know the minute Van Staub comes in, please.

01:08:21.120 --> 01:08:28.360
- Of course. Would you sit down? - Thank you.

01:08:32.590 --> 01:08:38.630
- Mr. Grayson, I don't understand. - Do you understand the penalty for treason, Dr. Neva?

01:08:38.710 --> 01:08:44.670
Treason? What is the purpose of this interview? What do you mean by treason?

01:08:44.750 --> 01:08:47.250
It would be much better if you cooperated with us, Dr. Neva.

01:08:47.340 --> 01:08:51.100
We know that secret information has been sent out of this plant to foreign agents.

01:08:51.120 --> 01:08:53.190
- Oh, it couldn't have. - But it has.

01:08:53.200 --> 01:08:59.480
Each Friday after the conferences, von Staub's calculations have been copied in a solution of silver salts.

01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:02.100
- From one of your handkerchiefs. - That's impossible.

01:09:02.200 --> 01:09:05.600
Later on the same evening, you've delivered that handkerchief to the elite laundry.

01:09:05.720 --> 01:09:07.620
Concealed among your other personal effects.

01:09:07.720 --> 01:09:10.290
- How long have you known this man Braun? - Braun?

01:09:10.390 --> 01:09:13.570
- I don't know anyone by that name. - Come now, Doctor.

01:09:13.710 --> 01:09:17.010
Surely you remember the name of the man for whom this information was intended.

01:09:17.110 --> 01:09:19.510
I've had enough of this. I won't be browbeaten.

01:09:19.610 --> 01:09:22.510
I don't know what your motive is, but I refuse to be treated like a criminal.

01:09:22.610 --> 01:09:27.600
How dare you insinuate that a handkerchief of mine could be connected with theft or treason?

01:09:27.770 --> 01:09:29.700
I'm not insinuating, Dr. Neva.

01:09:30.280 --> 01:09:34.320
- I'm stating a fact. - Mr. O'Hara, what you're trying to prove is absurd.

01:09:34.420 --> 01:09:36.520
Let me remind you that I am not a mathematician...

01:09:36.620 --> 01:09:38.220
and I do not understand the formulas.

01:09:38.320 --> 01:09:40.220
To copy them, you wouldn't have to understand them.

01:09:40.320 --> 01:09:43.540
If you have such a handkerchief, produce it. Prove that the handwriting is mine.

01:09:43.640 --> 01:09:47.230
I can do better than that, Doctor. We've never thought that you were in this alone.

01:09:47.330 --> 01:09:49.630
I'll prove that you have an accomplice here in the project...

01:09:49.730 --> 01:09:52.500
who furnished you with copies of the formulas and helped you deliver them.

01:09:52.770 --> 01:09:55.730
- An accomplice? - The man who created those formulas.

01:09:56.010 --> 01:09:58.920
If you're referring to Ritter von Stolbe, you're insane.

01:09:59.020 --> 01:10:00.000
After all, he's contributed...

01:10:00.030 --> 01:10:04.790
It's ridiculous to accuse him of betraying a country he's come to love.

01:10:04.870 --> 01:10:08.670
- Dr. Neva. - Wait a minute, O'Hara.

01:10:08.740 --> 01:10:11.250
We must give Dr. Neva a chance to collect her thoughts.

01:10:11.300 --> 01:10:14.170
After all, we're dealing with an intelligent, highly sensitive woman...

01:10:14.180 --> 01:10:16.270
who quite naturally is emotionally upset.

01:10:16.290 --> 01:10:21.610
I've seen Dr. Von Stolbe. I think he's charming.

01:10:21.640 --> 01:10:23.970
Oh, if you only knew him.

01:10:23.970 --> 01:10:25.940
I can understand how he'd attract any woman.

01:10:25.990 --> 01:10:28.640
Especially if he wanted to use her love to make a dupe of her.

01:10:30.280 --> 01:10:31.500
I don't think I understand you.

01:10:32.070 --> 01:10:33.010
But I understand you.

01:10:33.650 --> 01:10:35.190
You love him very much, don't you?

01:10:36.920 --> 01:10:39.590
Yes, I do love him very much.

01:10:40.690 --> 01:10:42.150
You understand, I want to be lenient.

01:10:42.840 --> 01:10:45.760
And I'm sure Mr. O'Hara would make a strong recommendation for clemency.

01:10:46.720 --> 01:10:47.540
Wouldn't you, Mr. O'Hara?

01:10:48.990 --> 01:10:51.210
I might, in return for full cooperation.

01:10:52.090 --> 01:10:53.570
There, you see, practically a promise.

01:10:54.570 --> 01:10:56.720
What a fool I am.

01:10:57.310 --> 01:10:59.250
I thought for a moment you believed me.

01:11:00.090 --> 01:11:01.790
You're very clever, Mr. Grayson.

01:11:01.890 --> 01:11:04.870
It's a pity I haven't a confession to make, even to you.

01:11:04.900 --> 01:11:06.820
Your performance deserves one.

01:11:06.940 --> 01:11:10.980
You didn't have to force me to admit that I love Dr. Von Stove. I do.

01:11:11.060 --> 01:11:12.160
So make the most of it.

01:11:12.260 --> 01:11:15.760
But I deny everything, every contemptible thing you've said or implied.

01:11:15.860 --> 01:11:17.410
And that's all I have to say.

01:11:33.640 --> 01:11:37.140
- Nice try. - Didn't do much good, though.

01:11:37.220 --> 01:11:40.140
- Did Dr. Neva... - For the time being, we're gonna confine her to her quarters.

01:11:40.220 --> 01:11:43.140
I'll assign a man to see that she doesn't leave the project.

01:11:43.220 --> 01:11:45.850
- She confessed. - What about Von Stoke?

01:11:45.930 --> 01:11:49.900
He hasn't reported to his office yet and doesn't answer his bungalow phone.

01:11:49.980 --> 01:11:53.730
- We were all up a little bit late last night. - Perhaps we'd better run over there, doctor.

01:11:53.810 --> 01:11:58.880
- I'll show you his quarters. - Thank you.

01:11:58.960 --> 01:12:00.000
I can't believe...

01:12:00.250 --> 01:12:05.750
like Von Stove would do a thing like this. I would have staked my life on his loyalty

01:12:05.750 --> 01:12:11.510
and integrity. He must have discovered that you were going to question Tony. His suicide

01:12:11.510 --> 01:12:17.500
is practically a confession. I guess you could interpret it that way. Well, it's a terrible

01:12:17.500 --> 01:12:21.890
thing to say, but if this puts an end to this whole ghastly affair... Do you know if he had

01:12:21.890 --> 01:12:27.050
any visitors last night? Yes, I know he did. Who was here to your knowledge, Doctor? Well,

01:12:27.050 --> 01:12:30.000
the conference lasted till about 11. I walked home.

01:12:30.060 --> 01:12:33.830
Tony dropped in after finishing her work.

01:12:33.930 --> 01:12:38.400
About 12:30, Forrest came in and Alan, we were all here.

01:12:38.500 --> 01:12:41.210
And shortly after that, Tony and I left.

01:12:41.310 --> 01:12:43.710
- Leaving Alan and Forrest alone with him? - Yes.

01:12:43.810 --> 01:12:48.480
- How was he dressed when you last saw him? - In his regular clothes.

01:12:48.580 --> 01:12:50.380
- Not in his pajamas? - No.

01:12:50.480 --> 01:12:54.050
I suppose you leave us to take care of the details, Doctor.

01:12:54.150 --> 01:12:56.340
Yes, of course.

01:13:03.100 --> 01:13:05.990
- Looks like a perfect suicide set-up, doesn't it? - Perfect.

01:13:06.090 --> 01:13:08.200
Guilty man likes courage to face arrest.

01:13:09.040 --> 01:13:14.030
Of course, that substantiates our case against von Stoven, Tony, right to the hilt.

01:13:14.110 --> 01:13:19.620
Certainly appears that way. Here's the glass used for the poison.

01:13:21.430 --> 01:13:26.280
That stuff is dynamite. Kills in a matter of seconds.

01:13:26.850 --> 01:13:30.000
Paralyzes the entire nervous system the instant it hits the throat.

01:13:32.300 --> 01:13:38.240
Say, can you imagine him drinking that here and then walking into there?

01:13:38.620 --> 01:13:40.040
That's a good question.

01:13:41.850 --> 01:13:42.850
Come here a minute.

01:13:57.240 --> 01:13:57.700
I see it.

01:13:58.400 --> 01:14:00.000
His glass couldn't have made that large a mark.

01:14:00.570 --> 01:14:02.690
There were two glasses here.

01:14:02.790 --> 01:14:05.460
Looks like our friend Von Stove had a late visitor.

01:14:05.560 --> 01:14:07.960
He couldn't have had his last drink and then removed the other glass from the table.

01:14:08.060 --> 01:14:09.860
And then walked into the bedroom.

01:14:09.960 --> 01:14:12.460
He was carried, of course.

01:14:12.560 --> 01:14:16.340
- It's murder. - It's got to be murder.

01:14:16.590 --> 01:14:18.490
The killer came back after the others had left.

01:14:18.590 --> 01:14:20.590
Von Stove got out of bed, went to the door and let him in,

01:14:20.690 --> 01:14:22.780
which means it was somebody that he knew very well.

01:14:22.880 --> 01:14:26.900
- Exactly. - And murder blows our whole case right out the window.

01:14:27.000 --> 01:14:28.760
Oh, what a mess I've made of this.

01:14:28.860 --> 01:14:30.000
I should get out of the office and tell them...

01:14:30.030 --> 01:14:31.920
to take me off the assignment.

01:14:31.960 --> 01:14:34.690
We're uncovered to the other side anyway, right out in the open.

01:14:34.770 --> 01:14:35.700
Oh, wait a minute.

01:14:35.720 --> 01:14:37.360
Braun has a problem too.

01:14:37.640 --> 01:14:39.190
By having Dr. Neva in custody,

01:14:39.270 --> 01:14:41.490
we've cut his line of communication with the Lakeview project.

01:14:41.760 --> 01:14:43.410
Yes, if it is Dr. Neva.

01:14:46.530 --> 01:14:48.690
But if it isn't Dr. Neva,

01:14:49.120 --> 01:14:52.010
Braun might be forced to have his collaborator inside the project

01:14:52.090 --> 01:14:53.810
bring that final formula out to him.

01:14:53.990 --> 01:14:55.160
What else can he do?

01:14:55.420 --> 01:14:56.640
You think he takes such a chance

01:14:56.720 --> 01:14:58.930
to risk of exposing the connection he has in the plant?

01:14:58.970 --> 01:14:59.830
He has to.

01:15:00.350 --> 01:15:05.360
He must have the final formula to make the information he already has of any value.

01:15:05.460 --> 01:15:10.320
The person we want inside the plant will lead us to the man we want outside.

01:15:10.420 --> 01:15:14.370
- I'd like to have another look at the film we made at the conference, wouldn't you? - Yes. Yes, I would.

01:15:14.470 --> 01:15:24.920
Mathematics is a pure science which doesn't permit fantasy, only fact.

01:15:25.020 --> 01:15:30.000
Eventually, all theory must be submitted to the proof of the law of numbers.

01:15:30.280 --> 01:15:32.480
on that paper in Von Staub's hand.

01:15:32.500 --> 01:15:33.300
All right.

01:15:35.190 --> 01:15:40.380
Which, I'm happy to say, has established our theory to be factual.

01:15:40.480 --> 01:15:41.000
Here.

01:15:41.010 --> 01:15:44.340
This is amazing.

01:15:44.610 --> 01:15:47.230
This is certainly the answer to our problem.

01:15:47.250 --> 01:15:50.190
Well, mathematics may be completely factual, as you say,

01:15:50.290 --> 01:15:53.390
but in your case, it's certainly blessed with a tremendous imagination.

01:15:53.490 --> 01:15:56.790
In the beginning, Einstein's entire thesis was imagination.

01:15:56.890 --> 01:15:58.290
I'm so proud of you, Ritter.

01:15:58.390 --> 01:15:59.390
Thank you, darling.

01:16:00.570 --> 01:16:02.760
Well, isn't our doubting Thomas convinced?

01:16:02.900 --> 01:16:08.330
And Stolbe, I don't quite understand the application of your second equation.

01:16:08.420 --> 01:16:10.150
Oh, I do. It's beautifully established.

01:16:10.250 --> 01:16:13.250
I may be mistaken, but it strikes me as being a minus factor.

01:16:13.250 --> 01:16:17.280
You have missed the inclusion of the element N3

01:16:17.280 --> 01:16:20.190
in the reconstruction of the nuclear pattern.

01:16:20.200 --> 01:16:25.280
Oh, yes. I should have known you couldn't be guilty of error.

01:16:25.380 --> 01:16:26.680
Oh, don't say that.

01:16:26.780 --> 01:16:30.000
Again, our mathematical Babe Ruth hits a home run.

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:34.120
- You'll take care of the notes, don't you? - Yes, of course, doctor.

01:16:34.200 --> 01:16:46.090
Well, I still can't figure it out.

01:16:46.170 --> 01:16:50.100
Neither can I. And the paper was always in plain sight.

01:16:50.180 --> 01:16:53.200
Except when Alan leaned across the table to check von Staub's results.

01:16:53.280 --> 01:16:55.360
Yes, but you could see it all the time.

01:16:55.440 --> 01:16:59.730
It stayed on that table until Tony took charge of it after the conference.

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:02.400
A copy of the formula was delivered to Braw on that same night.

01:17:05.020 --> 01:17:06.980
Continuing to hold Tony in custody,

01:17:07.580 --> 01:17:10.060
O'Hara, in cooperation with the local authorities,

01:17:10.320 --> 01:17:13.480
maintained a death watch over Townsend, Allen, and Forrest,

01:17:13.960 --> 01:17:16.060
who shared between them the final step

01:17:16.060 --> 01:17:18.440
in the successful completion of the Lakeview project.

01:17:19.310 --> 01:17:22.210
Both Grayson and O'Hara knew that there was no longer any doubt.

01:17:22.890 --> 01:17:24.770
One of those three men was the traitor

01:17:24.770 --> 01:17:27.460
and the murderer, Von Stoltz.

01:17:32.240 --> 01:17:33.640
Oh, her to Billings. Come in, please.

01:17:34.210 --> 01:17:34.750
Billings, over.

01:17:35.670 --> 01:17:37.950
California, 17U-982. Don't lose it.

01:17:38.710 --> 01:17:38.990
Check.

01:17:48.730 --> 01:17:54.310
You sure you won't take in that lecture with me?

01:17:54.960 --> 01:17:55.980
No, thanks. Not tonight.

01:17:56.920 --> 01:17:57.880
See you in the morning.

01:17:58.480 --> 01:17:58.870
Good night.

01:17:59.270 --> 01:17:59.640
Good night.

01:18:07.420 --> 01:18:10.560
- O'Hara to Grayson, come in. - Grayson to O'Hara, over.

01:18:11.000 --> 01:18:14.040
- California 29J422. - Right.

01:19:04.000 --> 01:19:06.810
O'Hara calling Grayson. O'Hara calling Grayson, come in, please.

01:19:07.110 --> 01:19:07.850
Grayson to O'Hara.

01:19:08.160 --> 01:19:10.340
Drop whatever you're doing and get right over here.

01:19:10.630 --> 01:19:13.060
I'm on Lakeview Boulevard, heading north towards Sepulveda Junction.

01:19:13.600 --> 01:19:14.990
Looks like I drew the lucky number.

01:19:15.730 --> 01:19:16.690
We found our man.

01:19:17.410 --> 01:19:20.230
And, Scotty, I know now how this thing has been done.

01:19:20.860 --> 01:19:23.140
I just found out what it was we missed in the projection room.

01:19:23.910 --> 01:19:25.330
Good boy. I'll be with you in a few minutes.

01:19:25.840 --> 01:19:28.080
And don't spare the horses. I'll fix your ticket.

01:19:32.650 --> 01:19:33.510
I heard all units.

01:19:33.970 --> 01:19:36.480
I heard all units in the Los Angeles police in this area.

01:19:37.060 --> 01:19:37.980
Emergency order.

01:19:38.720 --> 01:19:41.960
Converge on Lakeview Boulevard near Sepulveda Junction and await further orders.

01:21:04.510 --> 01:21:21.960
Grayson to O'Hara, come in. Grayson to O'Hara, come in, please. Grayson to O'Hara, come in, please.

01:22:00.700 --> 01:22:04.090
The wheel! My arms are pinned under the wheel.

01:22:17.480 --> 01:22:19.800
Thanks for dropping in. What happened?

01:22:19.800 --> 01:22:25.590
Let's get out of here. I'll tell you later.

01:22:25.590 --> 01:22:27.520
I heard all units. I heard all units.

01:22:27.520 --> 01:22:30.000
Locate and follow California license, nine.

01:22:30.060 --> 01:22:45.650
9P5017. I repeat. 9P5017. This car is escorted by a black sedan headed towards the public junction. Approach with caution. Do not apprehend or alarm these drivers. Allow them to reach their destination and report back to me.

01:23:09.720 --> 01:23:11.820
119 to O'Hara. Come in, please.

01:23:12.400 --> 01:23:14.360
O'Hara to car 119. Over.

01:23:14.910 --> 01:23:16.660
9P-5017.

01:23:16.860 --> 01:23:17.660
Trace to location.

01:23:18.280 --> 01:23:20.550
Location is isolated. House three miles north

01:23:20.550 --> 01:23:21.550
of Sepulveda Junction.

01:23:22.430 --> 01:23:24.250
Hearest intersection is Saugus Road.

01:23:25.020 --> 01:23:26.420
Instructions, please. Over.

01:23:26.900 --> 01:23:28.250
O'Hara to car 119.

01:23:28.250 --> 01:23:30.000
Keep location covered and permit no

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:52.380
to leave premises or her off car you once parked outside the house it was tailed in by that other

01:23:52.380 --> 01:23:56.480
car one man from the first car went into the house two from the other they're still in there

01:23:56.480 --> 01:24:00.000
they're still there sir no one came out good have your men get ready to move

01:24:00.120 --> 01:24:02.040
Don't fire unless I get the signal.

01:24:02.040 --> 01:24:13.620
- Right. - Braun, isn't it?

01:24:13.620 --> 01:24:14.470
Looks like him.

01:24:14.470 --> 01:24:19.120
- That's the last we saw of him. - You've done well, Comrade Krebs.

01:24:19.120 --> 01:24:20.120
You too, Ivan.

01:24:20.120 --> 01:24:22.680
In a few days, we shall be honored

01:24:22.680 --> 01:24:24.680
as none of our comrades has ever been honored before.

01:24:24.680 --> 01:24:27.050
We shall be able to ask anything.

01:24:27.050 --> 01:24:28.070
Anything.

01:24:28.070 --> 01:24:29.190
Braun!

01:24:33.390 --> 01:24:35.120
This is your last chance to surrender.

01:25:26.200 --> 01:25:30.000
Throw your guns out the windows and come out with your headphones.

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:31.370
Hands up!

01:25:31.370 --> 01:25:33.560
We... we haven't got a chance.

01:25:33.560 --> 01:25:53.300
That's my militia.

01:26:39.690 --> 01:26:42.040
- Nice shooting, Scotty. - Thanks.

01:26:51.360 --> 01:26:54.640
Scarabron and the boys, where's the one we really want?

01:27:12.090 --> 01:27:13.290
or should we come in?

01:27:13.290 --> 01:27:21.640
Just a few seconds.

01:27:21.640 --> 01:27:24.450
Now, look, Dr. Allen.

01:27:24.450 --> 01:27:26.450
But I tell you, I was not there of my own free will.

01:27:26.450 --> 01:27:28.450
Yes, I know, you've told us that a dozen times.

01:27:28.450 --> 01:27:30.000
At 6:30 this morning,

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:31.920
I received an urgent telephone call...

01:27:32.220 --> 01:27:35.280
asking me to come to that house on a matter of grave importance.

01:27:35.380 --> 01:27:37.340
When I arrived at the door, I was seized by those men...

01:27:37.440 --> 01:27:38.940
without so much as an explanation.

01:27:39.040 --> 01:27:41.620
Oh, I see. Practically kidnapped, eh?

01:27:41.720 --> 01:27:44.960
Why, yes. What could they have wanted from me? I have no money.

01:27:45.060 --> 01:27:46.760
We know what your friend Braun wanted.

01:27:46.800 --> 01:27:48.940
The final daughter on the Lakeview project.

01:27:49.010 --> 01:27:49.930
- Are you insinuating... - Shh.

01:27:50.030 --> 01:27:51.730
Sit down, Dr. Allen. Just keep your seat...

01:27:51.830 --> 01:27:53.430
until we can put you in a much hotter one.

01:27:53.430 --> 01:27:55.220
This is preposterous.

01:27:55.320 --> 01:27:56.740
I am a man of standing.

01:27:56.840 --> 01:27:59.570
I have an unblemished personal and professional reputation.

01:28:00.670 --> 01:28:04.590
Mr. North, may I inquire on what idiotic charge I am being held?

01:28:04.640 --> 01:28:07.480
Treason. Conspiracy against the people of the United States.

01:28:07.640 --> 01:28:10.050
And murder. Von Staub?

01:28:10.740 --> 01:28:12.800
You must have been under tremendous pressure, doctor.

01:28:13.080 --> 01:28:14.720
You weren't too clever with that affair.

01:28:15.000 --> 01:28:16.320
Can't you add a few more?

01:28:17.020 --> 01:28:19.720
Have you any idea what fools you're making of yourselves?

01:28:19.880 --> 01:28:23.450
- Oh, Dan, I'll say. - I demand proof of these absurd charges.

01:28:23.610 --> 01:28:26.500
I think I can satisfy even you, doctor. May I see your hand?

01:28:26.660 --> 01:28:29.100
I refuse to submit to these Gestapo practices.

01:28:29.260 --> 01:28:30.000
Aren't you gonna call us out?

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:33.260
There's fascist black shirts, too, doctor. Isn't that part of the usual routine?

01:28:33.340 --> 01:28:35.720
- I have influential friends in Washington. - Your hand.

01:28:35.800 --> 01:28:38.450
- I'll see that you sweat for this outrage. - Your hand, please, doctor.

01:29:02.910 --> 01:29:06.160
This is what we missed in the film of the conference at Lakeview, Mr. North.

01:29:06.350 --> 01:29:09.710
When this gentleman leaned across the table and pressed his hand on von Stobbs' formula,

01:29:09.790 --> 01:29:11.750
his palm had previously been treated with the chemical.

01:29:11.830 --> 01:29:14.650
Later, he developed the chemical, just as we did now.

01:29:14.790 --> 01:29:18.600
Then he made a copy of the formula on a handkerchief he stole from Dr. Neva's bungalow,

01:29:18.740 --> 01:29:20.540
which he later put back into a laundry hamper.

01:29:20.620 --> 01:29:22.260
Tonight, he didn't have that opportunity.

01:29:22.340 --> 01:29:25.210
He was forced to carry the information out to Brawn himself.

01:29:25.750 --> 01:29:28.200
Would you like to call your friends in Washington now, Doctor,

01:29:28.280 --> 01:29:30.000
and complain about the Gestapo?

01:29:30.380 --> 01:29:32.210
I wish to be represented by counsel.

01:29:32.230 --> 01:29:34.610
I demand the protection of due process of law.

01:29:34.700 --> 01:29:37.240
And the country you try to betray will see that you get it.

01:29:37.500 --> 01:29:39.600
I know my constitutional rights.

01:29:39.650 --> 01:29:41.360
You'll never convict me of anything.

01:29:41.550 --> 01:29:43.440
I'm no foreigner. I'm American.

01:29:44.600 --> 01:29:46.550
Yes, I know you are, Alan.

01:29:46.820 --> 01:29:49.240
We once had another American like you.

01:29:49.710 --> 01:29:51.480
His name was Benedict Arnold.

01:29:52.190 --> 01:29:56.340
- All right, Ward. - Come on.

01:30:04.570 --> 01:30:09.380
That's the end of Alan. Dr. Niebuhr, I want you to know how sorry we are.

01:30:09.550 --> 01:30:10.790
Thank you, Mr. Grayson.

01:30:11.530 --> 01:30:13.530
Sometimes in the line of duty, one is obliged to...

01:30:13.670 --> 01:30:18.980
I understand. Thank you for saving what Ritter von Stoeb gave his life to create.

01:30:20.250 --> 01:30:23.690
- Bye. Thank you. - Goodbye, sir.

01:30:23.850 --> 01:30:25.130
- Good luck. Goodbye. - Goodbye.

01:30:25.310 --> 01:30:27.260
I suppose now you'll be going home to England.

01:30:27.420 --> 01:30:30.000
Not a chance. If they want him, they'll have to bring Scotland Yard over here.

01:30:30.410 --> 01:30:32.610
Quite. Can't disrupt a good neighbor policy, you know.

01:30:32.810 --> 01:30:34.730
- Goodbye. - Goodbye.

01:30:36.050 --> 01:30:39.850
So international cooperation between two freedom-loving peoples...

01:30:40.050 --> 01:30:43.050
...wrote a successful end to our Lakeview case history...

01:30:43.430 --> 01:30:45.820
...which should bring us an added sense of well-being...

01:30:46.020 --> 01:30:49.820
...and knowing that the footsteps of those who walked the crooked miles...

01:30:49.970 --> 01:30:53.810
...are followed by such men as Grayson and O'Hara.
